DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This  function  should  never  be used.  Use ffrreeee(3) instead.  Starting
       with glibc 2.26, it has been removed from glibc.

   11‐‐aarrgg ccffrreeee
       In glibc, the function ccffrreeee() is a synonym  for  ffrreeee(3),  "added  for
       compatibility with SunOS".

       Other  systems have other functions with this name.  The declaration is
       sometimes in _<_s_t_d_l_i_b_._h_> and sometimes in _<_m_a_l_l_o_c_._h_>.

   33‐‐aarrgg ccffrreeee
       Some SCO and Solaris versions have malloc libraries with  a  3‐argument
       ccffrreeee(), apparently as an analog to ccaalllloocc(3).

       If you need it while porting something, add

           #define cfree(p, n, s) free((p))

       to your file.

       A  frequently asked question is "Can I use ffrreeee(3) to free memory allo‐
       cated with ccaalllloocc(3), or do I need ccffrreeee()?".  Answer: use ffrreeee(3).

       An SCO manual writes: "The cfree routine is provided for compliance  to
       the iBCSe2 standard and simply calls free.  The n and size arguments to
       cfree are not used."

